{"name":"Ginger Ale","hex":"#c9a86a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ginger ale","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A nonalcoholic drink flavored with ginger, similar to ginger beer."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ginger_ale"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ginger ale","blocks":[{"text":"Ginger ale is a carbonated soft drink flavoured with ginger. It is consumed on its own or used as a mixer, often with spirit-based drinks. There are two main types of ginger ale. The golden style is credited to the Irish doctor Thomas Joseph Cantrell. The dry style, a paler drink with a much milder ginger flavour, was created by Canadian John McLaughlin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Vernors_ginger_ale.jpg/330px-Vernors_ginger_ale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ginger_ale","wikidata":"Q1162763"},{"title":"Ginger","blocks":[{"text":"Ginger is a flowering plant whose rhizome, ginger root or ginger, is widely used as a spice and a folk medicine. It is an herbaceous perennial that grows annual pseudostems about one meter tall, bearing narrow leaf blades. The inflorescences bear flowers having pale yellow petals with purple edges, and arise directly from the rhizome on separate shoots."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Koeh-146-no_text.jpg/330px-Koeh-146-no_text.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ginger","wikidata":"Q35625"},{"title":"Ale","blocks":[{"text":"Ale is a style of beer, brewed using a warm fermentation method. In medieval England, the term referred to a drink brewed without hops."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6b/Real_Ale_2004-05-09_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ale","wikidata":"Q208385"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Canada_Dry_Pale_Ginger_Ale_Toronto_Star_ad_1916.jpg/500px-Canada_Dry_Pale_Ginger_Ale_Toronto_Star_ad_1916.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Canada_Dry_Pale_Ginger_Ale_Toronto_Star_ad_1916.jpg/500px-Canada_Dry_Pale_Ginger_Ale_Toronto_Star_ad_1916.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1916 Toronto Star ad for Canada Dry ginger ale","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanada_Dry_Pale_Ginger_Ale_Toronto_Star_ad_1916.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Yeast_and_nutrients.JPG/330px-Yeast_and_nutrients.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Yeast_and_nutrients.JPG/330px-Yeast_and_nutrients.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Yeast","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYeast_and_nutrients.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/8_Gl%C3%A4ser_Ginger_Ale.jpg/500px-8_Gl%C3%A4ser_Ginger_Ale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/8_Gl%C3%A4ser_Ginger_Ale.jpg/1280px-8_Gl%C3%A4ser_Ginger_Ale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/8_Gl%C3%A4ser_Ginger_Ale.jpg/500px-8_Gl%C3%A4ser_Ginger_Ale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Glasses of ginger ale with wedges of limes","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A8_Gl%C3%A4ser_Ginger_Ale.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Ginger_ale_bottles.jpg/330px-Ginger_ale_bottles.jpg?utm_source=en